  "interpretation": "## What changed\n\nOver the last 90 days, 2,916 users asked Excel MCP to carry out 837,077 meaningful actions. 60.34 percent used it on at least two different days. This suggests that many people continue using the project after first trying it.\n\nGrowth remained strong in the latest 14 days. Users increased from 1,107 to 1,280, a rise of 15.63 percent. Actions increased from 201,195 to 282,279, a larger rise of 40.3 percent.\n\n## How well it worked\n\nCategorized reliability begins with release 2.0.5. Earlier releases did not record the safe outcome labels needed to separate expected results from product failures, so their rows are not reclassified. There is not yet enough categorized data to describe release reliability.\n\n## How people use it\n\nTables and ranges account for 66.01 percent of meaningful actions. Worksheets and connections add 11.86 percent, while PivotTables and charts add 7.9 percent. Practical spreadsheet work is clearly the center of how people use Excel MCP.\n\nUse also reaches specialist features. VBA macros account for 5.88 percent of actions, Power Query for 4.4 percent, and Power Pivot and DAX for 1.86 percent.\n\n## What we will improve\n\nWe will use the new categories to separate caller-correctable results, Excel or outside-service problems, and Excel MCP faults. Unknown failures will remain visible rather than being guessed. Excel performance work is deliberately deferred unless later evidence shows a problem owned by Excel MCP.",
  "interpretationModel": "GitHub Copilot CLI"
}
